<Docs
  title="Repeat and scheduling"
  description="Recurring work is configured in a picker and shown back to you in plain language, so a rule you wrote months ago is still readable."
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="rules">Repeat rules</h2>
  <p>
    A task can repeat on a rule, and that rule is set through a dedicated picker rather than by typing a
    string into a due-date field. The task then carries the rule with it: the same item comes back when it
    is due, in the same place in the tree, instead of a copy you have to re-file.
  </p>
  <p>
    Repeats are a first-class part of the task model, not a calendar app bolted onto the side — the rules
    are stored alongside the tasks in the local database.
  </p>

  <h2 id="dates">Dates and times</h2>
  <p>
    Due dates go through the same date and time picker used everywhere else in the app, with a calendar to
    jump by month. Scheduling is deliberately a small set of explicit choices: a date, optionally a time,
    and optionally a repeat — which is enough to answer "when does this come back" without inventing a
    grammar.
  </p>
  <p>
    Rules cover daily, weekly (including specific weekdays), and monthly (including a day of the month)
    rhythms. Each new occurrence is generated from the rule when it comes due, in the same place in the
    tree.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sequences">Start times and sequences</h2>
  <p>
    A repeat can have a start time as well as a due time: the occurrence stays hidden until it becomes
    doable, then appears. Repeats can also be chained, so one occurrence of a rule waits on the same-day
    occurrence of another — "shower after gym", every day, in the right order. A repeat can even schedule
    a workflow run instead of a plain task, which is how recurring checklists stay automatic.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why a picker</h2>
  <p>
    Free-text recurrence is where task managers quietly go wrong: the rule is unreadable after a month,
    invisible when it misfires, and different every time someone types it. A picker trades a little speed
    at entry for a rule the app can display, edit, and reason about later.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="tip" title="Made to be reviewed">
    Rules that are visible are rules you will fix. If a task repeats more often than you meant, the picker
    is where you correct it — see <a href="/docs/tasks#details">Task details</a> for the fields a task carries.
  </Callout>
</Docs>
